# Build context exclusions for the Langfuse control-loops image.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DELETING A LINE. The image built from this directory is
# pushed to a shared ECR repository and runs a PUBLIC, unauthenticated app.
# Anything that lands in the context can land in a layer, and layers are
# readable by anyone with ECR pull access and by any file-read bug in the app.
#
# The workshop precedent at workshops/build_workshop/playbook/Dockerfile does a
# bare `COPY . .` with no .dockerignore. Copying that pattern here would have
# been a credential leak, not a style nit: see the .env note below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY EVERY PATTERN BELOW IS `**/`-PREFIXED. This is the bug this file had.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .dockerignore patterns are anchored at the CONTEXT ROOT, not matched against
# every path segment the way .gitignore matches a bare name at any depth. So a
# line reading `.env` excludes ./.env and NOTHING ELSE. A review seeded nested
# canaries against the previous version of this file and built it; all of these
# landed in the image with only root-anchored patterns:
#
#   /app/app/.env            OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-DOCKERIGNORE-CANARY-APPDIR
#   /app/app/.env.local      (matched by neither `.env` nor `.env.*`)
#   /app/app/prod.env        (`*.env` is also root-only)
#   /app/static/.env         OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-DOCKERIGNORE-CANARY-STATIC
#   /app/app/__pycache__/canary.pyc
#   /app/app/sandbox/pricing.py
#   /app/static/.claude/session.json
#
# Only the root ./.env was correctly excluded. Since app/config.py load_dotenv()s
# both this directory and its parent, a working copy putting .env next to the
# source is the expected shape, not a contrived one, which made this the single
# most likely way for this image to ship a live key.
#
# `**/x` matches x at every depth INCLUDING the context root in Docker's
# patternmatcher, so the recursive form is a superset of the anchored form and
# there is no need to keep both. Verified by rebuilding with the same canaries.
#
# When you add a line here, ask whether the thing can exist in a subdirectory.
# If it can, it needs `**/`. Almost everything can.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials. NOT clutter. Do not remove.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The upstream working copy this demo was snapshotted from keeps a real .env
# next to the source: a live 164-character sk-proj- OpenAI key and live pk-lf- /
# sk-lf- Langfuse keys. app/config.py also load_dotenv()s the PARENT directory,
# so a sibling .env is part of this app's normal search path and can plausibly
# exist in a working copy. Either file inside the context bakes live spend
# credentials into a pushed image, where a rotation is the only remedy.
#
# The deployed app needs none of this: credentials arrive per request in
# X-OpenAI-Key / X-Langfuse-* headers from the visitor's browser. An image that
# contains no credential is the whole security story here, so keep it that way.
#
# Three patterns, because they cover three different filenames: `.env` itself,
# the `.env.production` suffix form, and the `staging.env` prefix form. Dropping
# any one of them leaves a real filename shape unexcluded.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env

# Other credential-shaped filenames that a working copy accumulates. None of
# these belong in a container that authenticates to nothing.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/id_rsa
**/id_ed25519
**/.netrc
**/.aws/
**/.npmrc
**/credentials.json
**/service-account*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local state that must never become image state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sandbox/ is mutable runtime state written by loop 3 (coding agent). Shipping a
# developer's mutated copy would start every container mid-demo instead of on
# the seed file, and app/coding_agent.ensure_sandbox() recreates it on boot.
# Recursive because app/sandbox/ is at least as likely as ./sandbox/: the app
# resolves it relative to the package, so running uvicorn from either directory
# produces one or the other, and a developer cannot be expected to know which.
# The /app/sandbox in the image comes from the Dockerfile's own mkdir, owned by
# the demo user; that is the only copy that should exist.
**/sandbox/

# A macOS/arm64 virtualenv in a linux image is dead weight at best, and pip
# would not use it anyway. Dependencies come from requirements.txt.
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/ENV/

**/.git/
**/.gitignore

# Bytecode. This is not hygiene, it is a demonstrated leak path: README.md tells
# you to run the app locally before building, and doing so creates
# app/__pycache__, which the previous root-anchored `__pycache__/` did not
# exclude. Every developer who followed the README baked their own bytecode into
# the next image. PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE in the Dockerfile only covers the
# container's own runtime, not what the COPY picks up from the host.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.so

# Agent and tooling scratch dirs. .gstack and .claude can contain session
# transcripts and tokens; .chdata is local ClickHouse data.
**/.gstack/
**/.claude/
**/.chdata/
**/clickhouse-data/
**/scratchpad/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Noise: not dangerous, just wasted build cache and image size
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/*.log
**/*.swp
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov/

# Docs are read on GitHub, not in the container. Root-anchored on purpose for
# the three build files: there is exactly one of each and it is here, and a
# `**/Dockerfile` would also drop any Dockerfile a demo fixture needs. *.md is
# recursive because docs do nest.
**/*.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
apprunner-service.json
